    "media:imagetext": "Flying from Hongkong to Hankow\nOct. 25, 1937.\nDear Family:\nAm now on a trip which promises to be a very interesting one and\nso for my own amusement and to continue my long neglected family letters\n\"I'll write as I please.\"\nMr. Bond and I left\nHongkong at 11:55 this morning.\nWe are flying\nin a tri-motored Junkers operated by the Sino-German Eurasia Company.\nmen and two women, also a full load of air express including 175 lbs. of\nanti-toxin consigned to the American Ambassador at Nanking.\nThe latter\nshipment came over from the U.S. by Pan American and is carried free of\nIt is somewhat gratifying to one in\nthe business - temporarily resident in China - to see aircraft put to\ngood constructive use after viewing and reading about all the destructive\nand cruel uses to which airplanes have lately been put in china.\nOur take-off was bumpy as are most take-offs from the pocket-like\nHongkong airport surrounded by high mountains.\nClimbed through low\nground haze and broken white fluffy clouds at 3000 feet and now, having\ncrossed infamous Bias Bay (where the Chinese pirates have given way to\nthe more legal but equally piratical Japs), we are flying over Chinese\nterritory. Down below are rugged brown mountains completely deforested\nsides seared and torn by the rush of mad streams born of the torrential\nrains which come in the\ntyphoon season.\nIn the valleys small Chinese\nvillages furnish shelter for the coolle farmers who cultivate every inch\nof soil washed down from the mountains.\nThe little valleys look very\npeaceful and viewing them I can understand why many of our Chinese\nfriends have sent their families \"back to the country\". Those little\nvillages are not worth a Japanese serial bomb - no army occupation of\nthis country is even threatened and those little villages are probably\nsafer than ever before because the war lords are all busy and will not\nmarch their hordes of Chinese soldiers through this section.\nWe are well away from the coast now flying at 7000 feet - clouds\nhave all disappeared and an Arizona sky stretches from one haze covered\nhorizon to the other.\nWe have not sighted any other airplanes and I'll\nbe pertectly happy it none are encountered - of either side! Our pilot\nreports, nowever,\nthat the Hankow Field - our objective - was bombed\nlight before last by Japanese planes - hangar destroyed - three Chinese\nbombers on the ground and our poor old Western Division of PAA Ford\n\"loaned\" to the Chinese Army were all burned up.\nUnfortunately, the\nbombers were a German made Heinkle, an Italian made Savoia-Machett1 (sp !)\nand an American made Martin, all badly needed by the Chinese,\nand among\nthe last of the good planes\nthey had left.\nThe pilot also says the\nfield is full of shell holes but all holes are clearly marked with red\nflags and several runways are marked out for safe landing areas so there\nis nothing to worry about. Bond reminds me that it 1s now 1:30 and that\nour Chinese No.1 at Hongkong sent some lunch boxes to us, so we'll have\nchow and I'll continue this letter later on.\nLater - in fact after a good lunch of fried chicken, sandwiches,\nhot coffee and cake, I lit a cigarette, tilted my chair back, enjoyed\na smoke and took a short siesta.\nMy box also contained some cold salad\nbut I passed that up.\nThere is\nstill some cholera in Hongkong). It 1s\nnow 2:30 PM and we are now about 375 miles northwest of Hongkong\n-1-\n0330",
